---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TimeSeries from "../../charts/TimeSeries.jsx";

const REPO = "https://github.com/MiguelPuntoEs/historical-monetary-data/tree/main/greenbacks";
const abs = (path) => new URL(path, Astro.site).href;

// Dataset markup makes these eligible for Google Dataset Search, where a
// transcription like this competes far better than in general web search.
const datasetLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Dataset",
  name: "The greenbacks, 1859–1866",
  alternateName: "Gold premium, prices, wages and money stock during the American Civil War",
  description:
    "Gold prices, commodity prices, wages and the money stock during the American Civil War, transcribed from page images of Wesley C. Mitchell's A History of the Greenbacks (1903) and the Friedman–Schwartz–Mitchell estimates in Historical Statistics of the United States.",
  url: abs("/data/greenbacks"),
  license: "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/",
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  creator: {
    "@type": "Person",
    name: "Miguel González Calvo",
    url: abs("/"),
  },
  temporalCoverage: "1859/1866",
  spatialCoverage: { "@type": "Place", name: "United States" },
  measurementTechnique: "Manual transcription from page images, cross-checked against a second source where one exists",
  keywords: [
    "greenbacks",
    "American Civil War",
    "gold premium",
    "money stock",
    "monetary history",
    "nineteenth-century prices",
    "United States",
  ],
  variableMeasured: [
    "Gold price of $100 in paper currency",
    "Money stock",
    "Non-interest-bearing government currency",
    "Specie stock",
  ],
  citation: [
    "Wesley C. Mitchell, A History of the Greenbacks: With Special Reference to the Economic Consequences of Their Issue, 1862–65 (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1903), Appendices A–C and Table V.",
    "Historical Statistics of the United States, Millennial Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2006), Table Cj26-41 — Friedman, Schwartz and Mitchell, contributed by Hugh Rockoff.",
  ],
  includedInDataCatalog: {
    "@type": "DataCatalog",
    name: "Historical monetary data",
    url: abs("/data"),
  },
  distribution: [
    {
      "@type": "DataDownload",
      name: "Gold price of paper, monthly",
      encodingFormat: "application/json",
      contentUrl: abs("/data/greenback-gold-monthly.json"),
    },
    {
      "@type": "DataDownload",
      name: "Money stock, specie and government currency",
      encodingFormat: "application/json",
      contentUrl: abs("/data/greenback-money-stock.json"),
    },
    {
      "@type": "DataDownload",
      name: "Full transcription, CSV",
      encodingFormat: "text/csv",
      contentUrl: REPO,
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title="The greenbacks, 1859–1866"
  description="Gold prices, wages, commodity prices and the money stock during the American Civil War, from Wesley C. Mitchell's History of the Greenbacks (1903). Open CC0 data."
  article
>
  <script
    is:inline
    slot="head"
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={JSON.stringify(datasetLd)}
  />
  <main class="page">
    <h1 class="page-title">The greenbacks, 1859–1866</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      In 1862 the United States suspended specie payments and issued an
      inconvertible paper currency. Wesley C. Mitchell's <em
        >A History of the Greenbacks</em
      > (1903) remains the standard quantitative account, and its appendices are
      among the richest price and wage records of the nineteenth century. This dataset
      makes them machine-readable.
    </p>

    <div class="doc">
      <p>
        <a href={REPO}>Repository and CSVs →</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Released under CC0. The two charted series are also served directly as
        JSON: <a href="/data/greenback-gold-monthly.json">gold price of paper</a>
        and <a href="/data/greenback-money-stock.json">money stock</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The gold price of paper</h2>
      <p>
        The clearest single measure of the greenback's depreciation is what $100
        in paper currency fetched in gold on the New York market. The repository
        also carries roughly 1,228 days of daily high and low quotations.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TimeSeries client:visible src="/data/greenback-gold-monthly.json" />

    <div class="doc">
      <h2>The money stock</h2>
      <p>
        Alongside the price series, the Friedman–Schwartz–Mitchell estimates
        from
        <em>Historical Statistics of the United States</em>. Three series carry
        the essential story: the money stock, the non-interest-bearing
        government currency that was mostly greenbacks, and the total stock of
        specie, which left circulation after payments were suspended at the end
        of 1861.
      </p>
      <p>
        One caveat carried from the source: from June 1861 to November 1864 the
        figures cover the Northern states only.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TimeSeries client:visible src="/data/greenback-money-stock.json" />

    <div class="doc">
      <h2>Also in the repository</h2>
      <p>
        Not every series is charted here. The repository additionally carries
        about 120 wholesale commodity series, farm product prices by city,
        government purchase prices, and retail prices by town — all verified
        against the printed page. The Aldrich Report wage series by industry
        and occupation is <strong>partly</strong> transcribed (establishments
        covering pages 470–490 of 470–517); the Tenth Census wage series is
        not yet transcribed at all. See the repository's
        <code>FINDINGS_TRANSCRIPTION.md</code> for exactly what's verified.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Wesley C. Mitchell, <em
            >A History of the Greenbacks: With Special Reference to the Economic
            Consequences of Their Issue, 1862–65</em
          > (Chicago: University of Chicago Press, 1903), Appendices A–C and Table
          V.
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>Historical Statistics of the United States</em>, Millennial
          Edition (Cambridge University Press, 2006), Table Cj26-41 — Friedman,
          Schwartz and Mitchell, contributed by Hugh Rockoff.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Underlies <em
          >Revisiting Inflation in the American Civil War: Alternative
          Explanations Beyond Money Supply Growth</em
        > (APEE, 2026).
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
